  [38;5;153mindex.html[39m loading [38;5;153mapp.js[39m as a plain global script. No bundler, no ES modules, no tests, no linting. So "across the
  app" means across page loads, which rules out keeping the id in a variable.

  Next question: how long the id should survive.

  The fork: what event clears it. All three "persist," but they differ in when a stale id stops being attributed to the
  current person — which, for tracking data, is the difference between accurate attribution and quietly logging the
  wrong user.

  - [1mTab session[0m ([38;5;153msessionStorage[39m) — survives reloads and navigation between pages; gone when the tab closes. Matches the
    natural meaning of "who logged in during this visit." A second tab starts clean, which is either correct or mildly
    annoying depending on your flows.
  - [1mUntil explicitly cleared[0m ([38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m) — survives browser restart. Buys continuity across visits without re-login.
    Costs accuracy: on a shared machine, or after someone stops using the app, events keep getting attributed to the
    last id stored. It also means a user identifier sits on disk indefinitely, which is a privacy consideration worth
    being deliberate about even for tracking data.
  - [1mSingle page load[0m (in-memory) — no storage, no privacy surface at all. But every navigation loses it, so "other forms
    will need it later" wouldn't work. I mention it only to rule it out explicitly.

  My recommendation is tab session. Your goal is attributing actions to a login, and a login is a visit — [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m
  outlives the thing it describes, and the failure mode is silent wrong data rather than obvious missing data.
